{"gid":"2026_03_LV_NO","week":3,"away":"LV","home":"NO","away_col":"#C9D2DC","home_col":"#D9BE72","spread":3.0,"total":42.5,"fav":"NO","hpts":23,"apts":20,"kickoff":"Sun Sep 27 \u00b7 16:25","roof":"Dome","report_html":"<div class=\"report\"><p>NO are soft against the run (132 yds/game allowed), and LV feed <strong>Ashton Jeanty</strong> a heavy <strong>61%</strong> carry share \u2014 a rushing-yards spot.</p><p>The <strong>NO</strong> host the <strong>LV</strong> as favorites of <strong>3.0</strong>, with the total set at <strong>42.5</strong> \u2014 a market that implies a <strong>NO 23\u201320</strong> scoreline. Running the game forward from that baseline, we expect a contest that spends roughly <strong>31% of its snaps with NO in front</strong>, <strong>41% within one score</strong>, and <strong>27% with NO chasing</strong>. That distribution is the engine of everything below: game state is what turns a team's tendencies into a specific player's workload, so before we get to individuals it's worth being explicit about <em>how</em> this game is likely to be played.</p><p>This is a <strong>coin-flip script</strong>. With only 3.0 points separating them, neither team projects to spend much time in a blowout state \u2014 most of the game lives in the 'close' bucket, where play-calling stays balanced and usage tracks each player's established role rather than being distorted by score. In tight games the stars on both sides see their normal, dependable workloads, and there is less garbage-time leakage to backups.</p><h3>The Key Matchups</h3><p><strong>Tre Tucker</strong> faces a tough cover \u2014 NO hold WR1s to just 51 yds/game (vs ~58); a rec-yards under lean.</p><h3>Touchdown Board</h3><p><strong>Juwan Johnson</strong> is a live anytime-TD dart \u2014 LV are leaky to tight ends in the end zone (0.32 TE TDs/game vs ~0.24).</p><p><strong>Chris Olave</strong> carries TD equity \u2014 LV surrender 0.44 WR1 receiving scores a game.</p><p><strong>Tre Tucker</strong> carries TD equity \u2014 NO surrender 0.47 WR1 receiving scores a game.</p><p>In the red zone LV throw it (59% pass inside the 20) \u2014 <strong>Tre Tucker</strong>'s TD equity over a goal-line back.</p><h3>Game Script, Pace & Environment</h3><p><strong>Indoors</strong> \u2014 a dome removes weather variance and historically nudges passing efficiency up; a small tailwind for the passing games.</p><h3>NO \u2014 a <em>run-heavy</em> offense</h3><p>NO are one of the league's more run-committed offenses: on early downs while <strong>leading</strong>, they pass just <strong>37%</strong> of the time, against a league average of 44% \u2014 a genuine clock-bleeding identity, not noise (it holds after shrinking their sample toward the league).</p><p><strong>Travis Etienne</strong> (13 carries, 57 yards) projects to his baseline workload \u2014 no strong script push either way in a close game.</p><p><strong>Chris Olave</strong> projects for <strong>17 targets and 145 yards</strong>, in line with his established role in a script that neither inflates nor suppresses the pass.</p><p>Underneath, <strong>Juwan Johnson</strong> (TE, 9 targets, 75 yards) is the dependable secondary read \u2014 and note the red zone stays TE-friendly regardless of score, so his touchdown equity holds up even if the game script shifts.</p><h3>LV \u2014 a <em>balanced</em> offense</h3><p>LV are a balanced offense \u2014 their run/pass split by game state tracks the league closely (46% pass when leading vs 44% average), so their players' usage is driven more by role than by any strong situational lean.</p><p><strong>Ashton Jeanty</strong> (22 carries, 83 yards) projects to his baseline workload \u2014 no strong script push either way in a close game.</p><p><strong>Tre Tucker</strong> projects for <strong>11 targets and 85 yards</strong>, in line with his established role in a script that neither inflates nor suppresses the pass.</p><p>Underneath, <strong>Brock Bowers</strong> (TE, 10 targets, 80 yards) is the dependable secondary read \u2014 and note the red zone stays TE-friendly regardless of score, so his touchdown equity holds up even if the game script shifts.</p><h3>What to watch</h3><p>The single biggest swing factor is <strong>availability</strong> \u2014 use the injury tool in the sidebar to mark anyone out; a starter's absence doesn't just remove his line, it <em>redistributes</em> his targets and carries to specific teammates, and the report and projections rebuild around it. Beyond that: if this game breaks toward the projected 3-point margin, the <strong>garbage-time backups</strong> (the \u25c6-flagged rows below) are where soft lines and real edge tend to appear \u2014 those depth players get fed in blowouts, and the market is slowest to price them.</p><p><em>This preview and the projections below regenerate through the week as injuries, weather and line moves update the inputs.</em></p></div>","teams":[{"team":"LV","col":"#C9D2DC","players":[{"name":"Kirk Cousins","pos":"QB","starter":true,"minor":false,"markets":[{"label":"Pass yards","proj":203.5,"book":211.5,"over_th":190.76,"under_th":213.26,"over_am":"\u2212114","under_am":"\u2212115","pover":46,"call":"none","ev":1.4,"edge":-8.0,"skew":false},{"label":"Pass TDs","proj":1.4,"book":1.5,"over_th":1.0,"under_th":1.38,"over_am":"+140","under_am":"\u2212190","pover":42,"call":"none","ev":0.9,"edge":-0.1,"skew":false}],"td":{"pct":10.7,"fair":"9/1","book":"16/1","value":true},"d":{"pass_yards":{"m":203.5,"q":[85.47,145.85,201.98,258.35,316.61],"line":211.5,"od":1.88,"ud":1.87},"pass_td":{"m":1.4,"q":[0.22,0.22,1.19,2.16,3.13],"line":1.5,"od":2.4,"ud":1.53},"rush_yards":{"m":7.42,"q":[0.0,0.78,5.11,11.62,18.18],"line":null,"od":null,"ud":null},"rush_att":{"m":2.87,"q":[0.0,0.84,2.0,4.33,5.5],"line":null,"od":null,"ud":null}}},{"name":"Ashton Jeanty","pos":"RB","starter":true,"minor":false,"markets":[{"label":"Rush yards","proj":83.25,"book":68.5,"over_th":68.45,"under_th":83.46,"over_am":"\u2212115","